  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Ivanhoe’s boom began with an iron furnace at the end of the 19th century, then a National Carbide plant opened in 1917, sparking rapid growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://scholar.lib.vt.edu/VA-news/ROA-Times/issues/1990/rt9004/900402/04020074.htm"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name appears in a two‑volume history published in 1990, with volume one titled “Remembering Our Past, Building Our Future.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://scholar.lib.vt.edu/VA-news/ROA-Times/issues/1990/rt9004/900402/04020074.htm"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the late 1800s, preacher Robert Sheffey cursed Ivanhoe after seeing houses of ill repute, fighting, and drunkenness, and legend says the town sank into the earth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bunkhistory.org/resources/a-cursed-appalachian-mining-town"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "National Carbide closed its plant in 1966 and New Jersey Zinc pulled out in 1981, prompting Ivanhoe residents to form a Civic League to revive the community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://scholar.lib.vt.edu/VA-news/ROA-Times/issues/1990/rt9004/900402/04020074.htm"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A price store once stood as a landmark in Ivanhoe, remembered by locals in the short documentary “The Curse and the Jubilee.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bunkhistory.org/resources/a-cursed-appalachian-mining-town"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Ivanhoe’s history book is unique because it has no single author; it was written by the people of Ivanhoe themselves.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
